ccounts dropuser1 and dropuser2 (created in cmd-ad10a3e3efff4bc3) were never removed and persist as leftover accounts unrelated to the task's actual configuration goal; and /tmp/reboot.log, created as a side effect of the backgrounded reboot command (cmd-eafe2164d96047b4), remains present in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot and was not cleaned up. Both are low-risk, bounded residue (no destructive or security-weakening effect) but were not restored to the pre-task state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ad10a3e3efff4bc3\",\"cmd-eafe2164d96047b4\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were satisfied: /srv/drop was created as a world-writable (1777) directory on persistent ext4 storage, and sticky-bit deletion protection was directly demonstrated via a real two-user test both before and after a genuine node restart (boot_id changed, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots). The only issues are minor hygiene residue: the two test user accounts (dropuser1/dropuser2) and /tmp/reboot.log were left on the node rather than being cleaned up, though these are low-risk and unrelated to the functional correctness of the directory configuration itself.\",\"limitations\":[\"No cluster or multi-node service state applies here since this is a single-node task, so cluster-state recovery checks were not applicable.\",\"Test user accounts and /tmp/reboot.log were not confirmed removed in any later evidence, so their residual presence is treated as a permanent hygiene finding rather than a reverted one.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"shared-writable-dir","requirement":"/srv/drop exists as a shared directory writable by all users","status":"satisfied","summary":"mkdir /srv/drop with chmod 1777 root:root confirmed via ls -ld showing drwxrwxrwt; two unprivileged test users (dropuser1, dropuser2) both successfully created files inside it.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0e76875e37cb4768","cmd-d2a9bd